## 1. Why thread-per-request wastes resources

In a thread-per-request model, a thread waits while the database or external API responds. During that waiting time, it uses memory but does no CPU work. With many I/O-bound requests, the application needs many blocked threads. Reactor uses a small number of threads and continues other work while I/O is waiting.

## 2. Reactor pattern

- Demultiplexer: watches many I/O events.
- Event loop: receives events and dispatches work.
- Acceptor: accepts new connections.
- Handlers: process read/write events.

Blocking an event-loop thread is harmful because that one thread cannot process other connections.

## 3. Reactive Streams interfaces

The four interfaces are `Publisher`, `Subscriber`, `Subscription`, and `Processor`.

Sequence: `Subscriber` calls `subscribe()`, Publisher calls `onSubscribe()`, Subscriber calls `request(n)`, Publisher sends up to `n` `onNext()` signals, then sends `onComplete()` or `onError()`.

`request(n)` provides backpressure: it tells the publisher how many items the subscriber can handle.

## 4. Mono and Flux

`Mono` emits zero or one item. `Flux` emits zero to many items.

Nothing happens until `subscribe()` because Reactor pipelines are lazy.

`Mono.just(buildReport())` calls `buildReport()` immediately.  
`Mono.fromCallable(() -> buildReport())` calls it later, when subscribed.

## 5. map, flatMap, concatMap, and flatMapSequential

- `map`: function returns a normal value; keeps order.
- `flatMap`: function returns a Publisher; inner calls may run concurrently; output order is not guaranteed.
- `concatMap`: function returns a Publisher; runs one inner Publisher at a time; keeps order.
- `flatMapSequential`: inner calls may run concurrently, but output keeps source order.

## 6. Error handling

A `try/catch` around pipeline creation does not catch asynchronous errors because errors happen later, after subscription.

- `onErrorReturn`: return a fallback value.
- `onErrorResume`: switch to another Publisher.
- `onErrorMap`: convert one exception to another.
- `retryWhen`: retry according to a rule.
- `timeout`: fail if no result arrives within a specified time.

## 7. publishOn and subscribeOn

`subscribeOn` changes the scheduler used for subscription and upstream work.  
`publishOn` changes the scheduler for downstream operators.

A blocking JDBC call should use `Schedulers.boundedElastic()`.

## 8. Backpressure

Backpressure lets a slow subscriber control how much data it receives by calling `request(n)`.

If a source such as `Flux.interval` cannot slow down, use strategies such as buffering, dropping items, keeping only the latest item, or producing an error.

## 9. Cold and hot publishers

A cold publisher starts work separately for each subscriber. Therefore, subscribing twice to the same `WebClient` Mono sends two HTTP requests.

A hot publisher shares emitted data among subscribers.

`cache()` stores and replays the result for later subscribers.  
`share()` shares one active subscription, but does not replay old data to later subscribers.

## 10. Spring MVC vs Spring WebFlux

Spring MVC commonly uses blocking, thread-per-request processing. Spring WebFlux uses non-blocking reactive processing and event loops.

Spring MVC with virtual threads can be better when the application uses blocking libraries such as JDBC, has simple synchronous code, or does not need reactive streams end-to-end. WebFlux is better for high-concurrency non-blocking I/O workloads.
